b/ref 17592186422695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1069 "q-184242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1071 "Prove that if $n$ is odd, then $15n+3$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=\\,${{field-1}} for {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15n+3 & ={{field-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-4}}. Since $a$ is {{field-5}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-6}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15n+3 & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $15n+3$ is {{field-8}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15ce914f-28d4-58cf-a9d9-4237499cad54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422737 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,15n+3\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into the expression $15n+3$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15n+3 & =15(2a+1)+3 \\\\  & =30a+15+3 \\\\  & =30a+18 \\\\  & =2{{(15a+9)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=15a+9$ (highlighted above), we have that $15n+3=2b,$ which shows that $15n+3$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=15a+9.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15n-3 & =2{{(15a+9)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $15n-3$ is even." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1069 "q-184162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\nIf $n$ is an even integer, then ${n}^{3}+2n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $n$ be an {{field-1}} integer. Then, $n=$ {{field-2}} for some integer $a.$\n\nSubstituting {{field-3}} into the expression ${n}^{3}+2n$, we can show that ${n}^{3}+2n=$ {{field-4}}, where $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}+2n$ {{field-6}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}+2n$ is {{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24483f16-f633-5136-92ad-93ac714083a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422738 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{3}+2n\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nLet $n$ be an $\\text{even}$ integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{3}+2n$ and show it is *even* by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nSubstituting $n=2a$ into the expression ${n}^{3}+2n$, we can show that ${n}^{3}+2n=2b,$ where $b$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}+2n$ $\\text{is}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}+2n$ is $\\text{even}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1069 "q-184180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1071 "Prove that ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is odd if $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2n-3={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is {{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422739 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "594c61de-dccf